course: dictionary
lesson: 2611
title: Derail: trains and plans
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Derail is a verb, so it shows an action. Its first meaning is about trains. When a train derails, its wheels come off the rails. «Putting coins or anything else on the track is dangerous and can derail a train.» The sentence tells you that something on the track knocks the train off its rails.
explain: Derail also has a second meaning, used for plans and conversations. To derail a plan is to stop it going the way it should. «The bad weather derailed our picnic» is not about trains at all. The picnic did not go the way it was meant to.
explain: Use the context to choose the right meaning. If the sentence is about a train, it is about the wheels and rails. If it is about a plan or a talk, it means the plan or talk was stopped or spoiled. To check, swap in the words stop or knock off course and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Derail means to knock a train off its rails, or to stop something going the way it should.
fact: «The train derailed» means its wheels came off the rails.
fact: «Derail a plan» means to stop the plan going the way it should.
fact: Verb forms: derail, derails, derailed, derailing.
fact: Never put things on railway lines: they can derail a train, and people on it can be hurt.
exercise: «Putting coins on the track is dangerous and can derail a train.» What does derail mean here? | Paint the train a new colour | Make a train go faster | *Knock a train off its rails | Stop people buying tickets
exercise: Mum says, «The bad weather derailed our plan for the picnic.» What does derailed mean? | Wrote the plan down neatly | Made the plan longer | *Stopped the plan going the way it should | Finished the plan early
exercise: Find the past-tense form of derail in «The train derailed near the bridge.» | derail | bridge | near | *derailed
test: «Ella's conversation got derailed when the argument started.» What does derailed mean? | Got louder and faster | Finished early and happily | *Stopped going the way it should | Moved on to the next topic
test: Which rule matches the word derail? | *Never put things on railway tracks, because they can derail a train | Put coins on the track to see them flattened | Run along the edge of the platform | Always wave at the driver of a train
exam: Which sentence uses derail to mean stopping a plan going the way it should? | Derail the rails with a big hammer | The train derailed on the bend, and the wheels came off | *The argument derailed our plans for the day | The coins were flattened on the rails
